\section{Parameterization}{
The observed reaction time is \code{ndt + LogNormal(mu, sigma)}, so \code{mu} and
\code{sigma} are the mean and SD of the \emph{decision time} on the log scale, and the
median reaction time is \code{ndt + exp(mu)}.

\code{ndt} is expressed \strong{directly, in seconds} (through a log link in the \code{brms}
family). Nothing about it is taken from the data: it is not bounded by the
fastest observed response, so a non-decision time that varies by condition or
by participant can exceed the sample minimum wherever the data support it.
}

\section{The outlier component}{
A shifted distribution assigns exactly zero density to any response faster
than \code{ndt}, which puts a hard boundary in the likelihood at the fastest
observed RT. Mixing in a component with support over the whole positive line
removes it: every response keeps positive density whatever \code{ndt} is, so the
boundary becomes a finite cost rather than a wall and the log-density stays
smooth and differentiable. That is what makes the direct parameterization of
\code{ndt} workable without taking a bound from the data.

The outlier component is a \strong{half Student-t} with \code{3} degrees of freedom and
scale \code{minrt}, i.e. \code{2 * dt(x / 0.3, df = 3) / 0.3} on \verb{[0, Inf)} at the
default. Two properties motivate the shape. It is \strong{flat at the origin}
(zero derivative), so the very fastest responses - the ones least plausibly
decisions - are not starved of density; a LogNormal or Gamma vanishes at zero
and an Exponential peaks there with maximal slope, and all three get this
backwards. And its tails are heavy, so it keeps positive density across the
whole plausible RT range rather than only in the fast region. Plot it with
\code{curve(2 * dt(x / 0.3, 3) / 0.3, 0, 3)}.
}

\section{\code{minrt}}{
\code{minrt} is the \strong{fastest reaction time that could plausibly be a real
decision} - a conceptual floor for the task, not an observed statistic. It
is used directly as the scale of the outlier component, so it fixes what
"too fast to be a decision" means.

It is a judgement about the task, not a statistic: nothing is read off the
sample, and \code{ndt} is not bounded by it. \code{minrt} only sets where the outlier
component has its mass.

Because it is the scale itself, the component's shape relative to it is
fixed whatever value you choose: \strong{61\%} of the outlier mass falls below
\code{minrt}, and the density there is still 66\% of its peak. The default of
\code{0.3} seconds suits keypress paradigms - the conditional accuracy functions
in the \emph{Outliers} article show responses at chance below about 0.30 s across
three very different tasks.

It is \strong{not estimated}: it is a constant that fixes the timescale, in the
same way that the number of response options is a constant in an ordinal
model. It rarely needs touching, except when reaction times are not in
seconds:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{f <- brms::bf(RT ~ 1, sigma ~ 1, ndt ~ 1, poutlier ~ 1,
              family = cogmod_lognormal(minrt = 300)) # milliseconds
brms::brm(f, data = df,
          prior = cogmod_priors(f, df),
          stanvars = cogmod_stanvars(f),
          init = cogmod_inits(f, df))
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Put the family on the formula and name it only there: \code{minrt} has to reach
the Stan code, the prediction methods and the priors, and carrying it in one
place is what stops them drifting apart. \code{\link[=cogmod_priors]{cogmod_priors()}},
\code{\link[=cogmod_stanvars]{cogmod_stanvars()}} and \code{\link[=cogmod_inits]{cogmod_inits()}} all read it off the formula.

Leaving it at \code{0.3} with millisecond data is a silent failure rather than a
loud one: the component ends up many orders of magnitude below the decision
density everywhere in the data, \code{poutlier} collapses toward zero, and \code{ndt}
reverts to being pinned by the fastest observed response. Setting \code{minrt} in
the unit of the data makes the likelihood exactly equivariant to that unit,
so \code{ndt} comes back in whatever unit was supplied and \code{poutlier} is
unchanged.

\code{poutlier} is a \emph{rate}, not a classification: the model never labels
individual trials, it estimates what share of them came from elsewhere. Use
\code{\link[=p_outlier]{p_outlier()}} for per-trial posterior probabilities.
}

\section{Trimmed data: pin \code{poutlier} down, but not to zero}{
\code{poutlier} is only weakly identified when there is little to identify it
\emph{from} - which is why \code{\link[=cogmod_priors]{cogmod_priors()}} gives it an informative prior rather
than leaving it flat. If the data have already been trimmed, or only a
handful of implausibly fast responses remain, it is reasonable to stop asking
the data to estimate a rate at all.

The right way to do that is a \strong{very tight prior near zero}, not a hard zero:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{f <- brms::bf(RT ~ 1, sigma ~ 1, ndt ~ 1, poutlier ~ 1,
              family = cogmod_lognormal())
priors <- c(
  cogmod_priors(f, df),
  brms::prior(normal(-7, 0.5), class = "Intercept", dpar = "poutlier"),
  replace = TRUE
)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\code{normal(-7, 0.5)} on the logit scale is centred at about 0.09\%, with 95\% of
its mass between 0.03\% and 0.24\% - small enough to assert "there is
essentially no contamination here", while leaving the rate free to rise if
the data insist.

Fixing it outright is also possible, with \code{poutlier = 0} in the \code{bf()}, which
makes \code{brms} treat it as a constant and reduces the model to the plain
shifted family. \strong{Prefer the tight prior.} At exactly zero the density is
once again exactly zero below \code{ndt}, so the hard min-RT boundary returns and
\code{ndt} is pinned by the fastest observed response - which is the very problem
the outlier component was introduced to solve, reintroduced deliberately. A
rate of 0.1\% is numerically negligible for every other purpose but still
keeps the density positive below \code{ndt}, so the likelihood stays smooth and
\code{ndt} stays free.

Trim first either way. Neither option makes slow contaminants safe: those are
confounded with the right tail and bias \code{ndt} upward, so filter them before
fitting.

Slow outliers are deliberately not handled by this component. A slow
contaminant is statistically confounded with the right tail of the RT
distribution itself, so it cannot be identified, and leaving such trials in
the data biases \code{ndt} upward. Filter implausibly slow responses before
fitting.
}

\section{Predictions exclude the outlier component}{
\code{posterior_predict()} and \code{posterior_epred()} describe the \strong{decision process
alone} by default, as if \code{poutlier} were zero. For visualising effects the
outlier component is a nuisance that pulls expected values toward its own mean
and adds a spike of implausibly fast draws; it is also a fixed regularizer
rather than a claim about how guesses are distributed, so simulating from it
means simulating from something the model does not assert.

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{brms::posterior_epred(m)
modelbased::estimate_means(m, by = "Condition")
marginaleffects::avg_predictions(m, by = "Condition")
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Use \code{\link[=with_outliers]{with_outliers()}} for the fitted mixture, and \code{\link[=without_outliers]{without_outliers()}} to go
back. The one case that genuinely wants the mixture is a posterior predictive
check, since on untrimmed data the decision-only predictive has no fast spike
to match the one in the data:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{brms::pp_check(with_outliers(m))
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

The same flag can be set up front, with \code{cogmod_lognormal(predict_outliers = TRUE)}.

The flag is carried on the model rather than passed as an argument for a
reason. \code{brms} sends the \code{...} of \code{posterior_predict()} and
\code{posterior_epred()} to \code{prepare_predictions()}, not down to the family method;
\code{posterior_epred} reaches the family method with \code{prep} and nothing else. So
\code{posterior_epred(m, predict_outliers = TRUE)} is \emph{silently ignored} rather
than erroring, and \code{insight}, \code{modelbased} and \code{marginaleffects} inherit that
behaviour. Carrying the flag on the object is what makes it work everywhere.

The \code{predict_outliers} argument on the methods themselves still works when
they are called directly, and overrides the flag.

\code{log_lik} has no such argument: the likelihood \emph{is} the mixture, and dropping
a component from it would not be a different summary of the same model but a
different model. One consequence is that \code{posterior_predict()} and \code{log_lik()}
do not describe the same distribution by default. This also desyncs
\code{loo_pit()}, \code{loo_predict()} and \code{bayes_R2()} from \code{loo()}, not just
hand-rolled checks - anything that compares a simulated replicate against the
likelihood should be run on \code{\link[=with_outliers]{with_outliers()}}.
}

